WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:01.690
Hey David.

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:04.050
When do primary poles start to mean something?

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:09.039
Um, this came in from a lot of people, including
from Austin, Wenger via Twitter.

00:00:09.039 --> 00:00:14.949
In other words, when can we look at pot primary
polling data and historically say, now this

00:00:14.949 --> 00:00:19.850
is probably giving us a good sense of when
of who the nominees are ultimately going to

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:20.850
be.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.850
Let's just look at the data.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.850
Okay.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.360
We're currently 12 or 13 months out from the
conventions, which are in July and August

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.490
of next year.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:31.070
We're about 17 months out from the 2020 election.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:35.829
So this is like equivalent to early July,
2015 for the 2016 election.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:41.829
If you go back to July 5th, 2015 Donald Trump
was only polling six and a half percent.

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:47.579
So the idea there is you really aren't going
to know in July of the year before who's going

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:48.579
to win.

00:00:48.579 --> 00:00:49.579
The primary.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.069
Trump was polling only six and a half percent
in July, early July of 2015 he eventually

00:00:54.069 --> 00:00:55.300
won the nomination.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:00.780
However, by July 18th of 2015, Donald Trump
was tied for first place in the Republican

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:01.780
primary.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.820
Um, and from then on he was basically ahead
to the end with the exception of a couple

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:09.110
of polls in November of 2015.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:16.560
So on the republican side, historically by
late July, like two weeks from now, uh, you

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.960
would know or you would expect that the leader
in the primary polls would be the nominee.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.860
Now on the democratic side, if we look at
July five, 2015, which again is equivalent

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:33.850
to today, Hillary Clinton was polling 63 and
Bernie Sanders was polling 14.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:40.640
Now, What's interesting about the 2015 democratic
primary is that Hillary did lose that advantage

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.250
to Bernie all the way up to April of 2016
where Bernie got within five points of Hillary

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:47.250
Clinton.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:50.210
But then again, Hillary Clinton built that
lead back up.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.050
And as we know, she ultimately was the eventual
winner of the 2016 primary.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:02:00.090
So in 2016 primary, the eventual winner on
the democratic side, Hillary Clinton was leading

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.510
all along.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:07.140
But I know you will say, and I agree, 2016
in the DNC primary, there were certainly some

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.420
irregularities there.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:14.370
So what if instead we look at 2008, the reason
we won't look at 2012 is Obama was just the

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.000
incumbent and there was no a primary challenge
to him.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.750
Not a serious one that I can remember anyways.

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:27.290
So if we look at 2008 in July of 2007, which
is equivalent to right now, uh, Hillary Clinton

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:28.850
was ahead of Barack Obama.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:30.920
July 5th, 2007.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:37.910
It was Hillary 37, Obama 23 and of course,
eventually Barack Obama did win that primary.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:43.190
So that is the glimmer of hope that those
who don't want to see Joe Biden be the nominee.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:47.530
Like me, I don't, Joe Biden is not someone
that I'm supporting, uh, are looking at.

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:53.420
Now, as we know, uh, Barack Obama didn't take
the lead until February of 2008, which is

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.520
still several months from today, equivalently.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.700
So the takeaway is listen, in general, in
July of the year before the primary, certainly

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:08.380
by the end of July, almost always the person
who's leading the polls ends up being the

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.470
nominee.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:17.440
In cases where the July leader loses the lead
and does not get the nomination, um, the person

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.900
who does is not that far behind, they're polling
second.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.230
Maybe there's some examples of where they're
pulling third.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:30.441
So what this would tell us about 2020 is that
if it's not going to be Joe Biden who's the

00:03:30.441 --> 00:03:35.070
nominee, and again, statistically the most
likely thing is historically Biden is the

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:36.070
nominee.

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:40.950
If it's not going to be Biden history tells
us it will be someone who is already polling

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:47.260
well, which in my mind means if it's not Biden,
it's going to be Bernie or Elizabeth Warren

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:48.580
or Kamala Harris.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.980
Now there are those who say everything is
different in 2020.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:57.870
We can't go at all by what has happened historically
with these primaries.

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.270
And if that's the case, then throw everything
I set out the window and anybody could win

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:04.140
what I think everybody should be doing.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.430
Given the fact that it's likely we know where
this is going to go in terms of who is in

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:11.510
this thing, but that may not be the case.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:20.370
Vote for whoever aligns with your values on
policy, activate and campaign for those people.

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:25.810
That's all we can do and demand that the DNC
run this thing fairly, but that is where history

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:27.480
tells us.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.270
Polling starts to matter and it's basically
right around.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.101
Now, if you disagree with me, let

00:04:32.101 --> 00:04:33.101
me know.

00:04:33.101 --> 00:04:35.950
We've got a great bonus show in plenty more
time to discuss this there

